<!--Post: 2879--><!--Time: 1104224608-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2879"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:Cryo_rebirth@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Rhuakiel<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br><!--field:posts--><!--/field--><!--field:registered--><!--/field--><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 1:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->CVNCTAHAWYDAEAFNRLLBQDHIIHLM24BTS9NK24VPOIXYFWDMLZ PCCLZFVYHAKMSTP <BR> <BR>cawerldhlbnkpyfd <BR>Could be Carl or Call Earl or quite the number of things.  I so hope that you will include spaces in your messages next time BTK where I can tell what they&#39;re used for.  It won&#39;t mess up your message really. <BR> <BR>The post name was the key to it all.  Skip to the 4th, skip to the 4th, no skip... <BR> <BR>As for the 5A4B43 4B3530 70686F 746F42 544B, I believe that the post the faceupthe facedown&#39;s key was the ^ also used in grammar as the insert symbol.  So if you take the and insert it where it should fit &#40;By this I mean the kck are interchangable&#41;, you get &#34;kck 50photoz btk&#34; <BR> <BR>BTK, want to play online chess sometime?  I&#39;m quite interested to see how you think.  Perhaps checkers?  Is your name perhaps Earl or Carl?  Is that your next victim?  If so, I appreciate a confirmation if you would be so kind.  If I&#39;m even on the right track, I would be so happy if you confirmed even that bit.  Please? <BR>Anyhow, kudos on your cryptic skills.  Have you solved my little puzzle yet?  Using a library computer to e-mail me would be a nice way of communication if you like.  Anyhow, I&#39;ve taken ill so I must rest.  Let me know. <BR>Rhuakiel<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2879-->
<!--Post: 2882--><!--Time: 1104225429-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2882"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 1:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rhukiel, that&#39;s very interesting. <BR> <BR>An anagram of &#34;cawerldhlbnkpyfd&#34; is: <BR> <BR>&#34;Lynch dark fwd pleb&#34; <BR> <BR>Kind of neat that &#34;lynch&#34; is in there, and BTK is nothing if not &#34;dark.&#34;  According to an online dictinary I just checked, &#34;pleb&#34; means &#34;common people.&#34; <BR> <BR>&#34;Lynch dark forward common people?&#34;  Hmmm.  Probably nothing.  I&#39;m just playing with the letters.  <BR> <BR>I like your take on what the &#34;404&#34; meant.  That makes a lot of sense. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2882-->
<!--Post: 2906--><!--Time: 1104259878-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2906"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->dawgar<!--/name--></b></a><br><!--field:posts--><!--/field--><!--field:registered--><!--/field--><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 10:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rhuakiel  <BR>very interesting post: <BR>the most obvious meaning for kck is kansas city, ks...and the words photoz and btk were in the previous puzzle. <BR> <BR>hum...so out of 4B3530 70686F 746F42 544B you get kansas city kansas 50 photos btk... <BR>also kansas city kansas was in the cook airfield puzzle. <BR> <BR>good job<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2906-->
<!--Post: 2908--><!--Time: 1104261063-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2908"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tornado<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tornado<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 11:11 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I like this 440 solution better.  Go to 4th, Go to the 4th....then go to the letter O and repeat.  This is what you get. <BR>CAILF.....unscramble...you have CALIFORNIA.  Before all of the states went to 2 letter ID&#39;s, California was Calif.  I think he was born in CA, but has no connection to the Zodiac killer.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2908-->
<!--Post: 2928--><!--Time: 1104283473-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2928"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 5:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->You are stopping at the space, however.  If you follow it all the way to the end &#40;the space has been determined to be computer generated&#41; you get the following: <BR> <BR>CAWERLDHLBNKPYFLCLYMS <BR> <BR>Now, out of that, I see CA-world-HL-bank-copy-FL-climbs.  Anyone see anything else or different?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2928-->
<!--Post: 2929--><!--Time: 1104283836-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2929"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iq<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iq<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 5:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->i see &#34;landwehr&#34;. lead investigator <BR> <BR>iq<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2929-->
<!--Post: 2930--><!--Time: 1104283997-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2930"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 5:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->How about... <BR> <BR>&#34;See a word her bank pay for crimes.&#34; <BR> <BR>&#40;Obviously jumbled, but I think the L&#39;s are supposed to be R&#39;s.&#41; <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2930-->
<!--Post: 2933--><!--Time: 1104288926-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2933"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:Cryo_rebirth@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Rhuakiel<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br><!--field:posts--><!--/field--><!--field:registered--><!--/field--><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 6:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There&#39;s nothing that we can really do with this clue.  If it is all read by the sound of the letters, then it could be an infinite number of things.  A wasted clue?  I would doubt that he would do such a thing.  Maybe we&#39;re just misusing the 440?  Heck I even saw the possibility of Pink Floyd in the message.  Our best hope is to keep trying different methods of 440 or try something different all together.  If it isn&#39;t really BTK that posted on that forum, I&#39;m going to be so P&#42;&#42;&#42;ed!  The least he could do is give some info that wasn&#39;t released to the public so that Tom could verify him.  BTK, if you are reading this, you could at least give us another clue as how to decipher this.  440 could be used so many ways.  Of course, my message wasn&#39;t so different from your own.  But I&#39;m sure mine is more encrypted. <BR>Titled <i>The Gate</i>, can you decipher it now?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2933-->
<!--Post: 2935--><!--Time: 1104290233-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2935"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->ws<!--/name--></b></a><br><!--field:posts--><!--/field--><!--field:registered--><!--/field--><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 7:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->perhaps BTK is a music fan: <BR>  <BR>In music, pitch is the perception of the frequency of a note. For example, the A above middle C is nowadays set at 440 Hz &#40;often written as &#34;A = 440 Hz&#34;, and known as concert pitch&#41;, although this has not always been the case &#40;see #Historical pitch standards&#41;. Pitch is often cited as one of the fundamental aspects of music. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2935-->
<!--Post: 2936--><!--Time: 1104293388-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2936"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 8:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->ws, you are right - that is the modern tuning in use by western orchestras - as a professional classical musician that occurred to me, too. But I have to agree with those who feel it has more to do with some kind of radio frequency, specifically ham band usage.  <BR> <BR>Then again, the message could just be spurious and not worth the parsing.  <BR> <BR>If you take it for granted that BTK has a love of old-time fiddle music - bluegrass &amp; country, as suggested by info released on 11/30/04 by Wichita PD - then even if he&#39;s an amateur musician and familiar with &#39;concert a&#39;, he knows that folk/country musicians are not especially stringent with keeping their instruments tuned to the 440 hz level. <BR> <BR>I am still pretty suspicious of any post purporting to be from BTk, or inferring it is by what it says, but there&#39;s just no real way to know right now, either.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2936-->
<!--Post: 2944--><!--Time: 1104301619-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2944"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 10:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There is/was a 440 Magnum Engine &#40;and other 440 engines&#41;. <BR> <BR>Dirty Harry was based on The Zodiac Murders. <BR> <BR>Dirty Harry carried a 44 magnum.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2944-->
<!--Post: 2947--><!--Time: 1104305380-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2947"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsbarn<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsbarn<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 28, 2004 - 11:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, sort of following Rhuakiel&#39;s formula, one way to interpret the 440 as a puzzle pattern is to skip count letters by four &#40;this would be the first 4 in 440&#41;. You get: <BR> <BR>CAAN BIMT KPYM CFAT <BR> <BR>Then add an O as in Oscar after each group of 4 &#40;for the 40 in 440&#41; and you have: <BR> <BR>Oo, man. I&#39;m a copy cat of BTK or, Rastafarian, O, mon, I am a copy cat of BTK.  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
